+* **Pixel-art upscaling for the HUD** (`GL_UI_UPSCALE`, on). A small texture that only 2D draws sample (through mode:
+  a button glyph, a HUD icon) and that looks like pixel art (a palette texture, or at most 256 colours) is uploaded at
+  twice its size, made by MMPX ([`host/gpu/mmpx.hpp`](../host/gpu/mmpx.hpp); credit in
+  [SOURCE_PROVENANCE.md](SOURCE_PROVENANCE.md)). The guest's texture, its size register and the draw's coordinates are
+  unchanged, so a draw that samples it gets the same UVs and simply finds twice the texels. The pass is a CPU pass done
+  once per texture, so it is metered: `GL_UI_UPSCALE_BUDGET_US` (1500) is what one frame may spend on passes after the
+  first, a texture over it is drawn as the original and done a frame or two later; `GL_UI_UPSCALE_MAX_US` (3000) leaves a
+  texture alone when the earlier passes predict it would take longer than that. 0 removes either limit. Only the
+  HUD changes (checked with a mission's frames, with and without); `ge_replay --stats` prints `ui2x_textures`, `ui2x_us`
+  and `ui2x_deferred`.
+* **Programs linked before they are needed** (`GL_PREWARM`, on). Linking a program is some 50 ms on the Vita, and a
+  mission's first seconds meet a dozen new ones. The renderer keeps a list of the flag sets it has linked
+  (`<data>/shader_variants.txt`, header `# portablekit-gl-variants 1`, one hex flag set a line, appended as each links),
+  reads it, and also a shipped one (`<data>/shader_cache/variants.txt`) at start, and links them from
+  `warm_up_step()`: the split renderer calls it from the GL thread when the queue is empty (`GeWorker::set_idle_work`),
+  at once while the game boots and later only after a lull of 20 ms, and one program per call, so a frame is never held
+  up by more than one link. `GL_VARIANT_DIR` moves the folder. Off, no list is read or written. A list is only a hint:
+  a flag set the renderer cannot build is skipped, and one that is missing costs what it always cost.
 * **Blending without `glBlendColor`.** `GL_CONSTANT_COLOR` factors are used only where the driver has them and
   the setting `no_blend_color` is off; otherwise the fragment shader pre-multiplies (`u_blend.x`: 1 doubles the
   source alpha, 2 doubles and complements, 3 multiplies by a fixed colour, 4 a fixed colour against its
@@ -218,6 +236,16 @@ every use:
 
 `texture_always_validate=1` is the old behaviour (hash and compare on every use) for comparison.
 
+**Decoding.** `decode_texture()` turns a guest texture into RGBA8. A palette texture and a swizzled 16-bit one are read
+straight from guest memory through one pass that unswizzles, looks up the palette and expands in one go, without first
+copying the whole texture (`decode_indexed_fused`); the 16-bit formats expand through 32- and 64-entry channel tables
+instead of a multiply and a divide a channel; a swizzled texture is walked a 16x8-byte block at a time. On a Cortex-A9 this is what makes a
+level load's hundred-odd uploads (5-24 ms each before) shorter; on x86 the palette textures decode about 2.8 times
+faster. `tests/texture_decode_tests.cpp` compares every format (linear and swizzled, odd sizes, buffers wider than the
+texture, palette masks, shifts and offsets) with a plain reference written from the format rules, 1500 random
+textures, bit for bit. `<PREFIX>_NO_FUSED_DECODE=1` turns the fused path off, `<PREFIX>_NO_FAST_TEXTURE_DECODE=1`
+turns all of them off, and `<PREFIX>_CHECK_TEXTURE_DECODE=1` decodes both ways and reports any texel that differs.
+
 **HD replacements are read off the GL thread** (`HdConfig::async_load`, `<PREFIX>_HD_ASYNC=0` turns it off; the
 replayer always turns it off, so a replay has every replacement from its first frame). The first time a texture
 with a replacement is drawn, its original is uploaded and the pack's loader thread is asked for the replacement:
diff --git a/docs/SOURCE_PROVENANCE.md b/docs/SOURCE_PROVENANCE.md
index ae2c206..c7a66c2 100644
--- a/docs/SOURCE_PROVENANCE.md
+++ b/docs/SOURCE_PROVENANCE.md
@@ -26,6 +26,10 @@ The built-in server (`server.cpp`, used by *Host a session* and `--adhoc-server`
 
 `host/gpu/texture_pack.cpp` and `replacement_textures.cpp` load HD texture packs made for PPSSPP, in its `textures.ini` format. They were written for this project. The format was learnt from PPSSPP's public documentation and pull requests about texture replacement, from reading PPSSPP's GPL-licensed source to find the format's facts (the layout of a key, the hash seeds, how the palette and the texture's dimensions enter the key, the order in which wildcard keys are tried, the ini sections and options), and from the keys a real community pack for Monster Hunter Portable 3rd HD Ver. uses, which the implementation reproduces. Only those facts and constants were taken; no code was copied, adapted or translated line by line. PPSSPP's `quick` hash is not implemented. Hashing uses xxHash, and PNG files are read and written with stb_image and stb_image_write, all unmodified (table below).
 
+## MMPX
+
+`host/gpu/mmpx.cpp` and `mmpx.hpp` implement MMPX, the pixel-art magnification filter of Morgan McGuire and Mara Gagiu, "MMPX Style-Preserving Pixel Art Magnification", *Journal of Computer Graphics Techniques* 10(2), 2021 ([paper](https://jcgt.org/published/0010/02/04/)). They are taken from [TeamGDB/Yakumo](https://github.com/TeamGDB/Yakumo) (`profiles/mhp3rd/host/gpu/mmpx.cpp`, MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 PSPRecomp contributors and Yakumo contributors), with the namespace changed and nothing else. Yakumo wrote it from the rules the paper lists; the authors' reference implementation (MIT, Copyright 2020 Morgan McGuire & Mara Gagiu) was not copied by Yakumo and was not used here. `tests/mmpx_tests.cpp` is Yakumo's test.
+
 ## Profile code
 
 A profile owns its generated AOT corpus, address-specific lowering, HLE behavior and native fast paths. Those files stay in the profile's own repository, and generated code is never committed at all, so they do not become hidden dependencies of the generic framework.
